egoryarray\":[\"0.3\",\"1.0\",\"3.0\",\"10.0\",\"30.0\"],\"nticks\":null,\"ticks\":\"\",\"tickcolor\":null,\"ticklen\":4.3171440431714414,\"tickwidth\":0,\"showticklabels\":true,\"tickfont\":{\"color\":\"rgba(77,77,77,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":13.814860938148611},\"tickangle\":-0,\"showline\":false,\"linecolor\":null,\"linewidth\":0,\"showgrid\":true,\"gridcolor\":\"rgba(235,235,235,1)\",\"gridwidth\":0.78493528057662576,\"zeroline\":false,\"anchor\":\"x\",\"title\":{\"text\":\"standard error (log scale)\",\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":17.268576172685766}},\"hoverformat\":\".2f\"},\"shapes\":[],\"showlegend\":false,\"legend\":{\"bgcolor\":null,\"bordercolor\":null,\"borderwidth\":0,\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":13.814860938148611},\"title\":{\"text\":\"\",\"font\":{\"color\":\"rgba(0,0,0,1)\",\"family\":\"\",\"size\":17.268576172685766}}},\"hovermode\":\"closest\",\"barmode\":\"relative\"},\"config\":{\"doubleClick\":\"reset\",\"modeBarButtonsToAdd\":[\"hoverclosest\",\"hovercompare\"],\"showSendToCloud\":false},\"source\":\"A\",\"attrs\":{\"1c18297b7e65\":{\"x\":{},\"y\":{},\"colour\":{},\"type\":\"scatter\"},\"1c1815747091\":{\"x\":{},\"y\":{},\"colour\":{}}},\"cur_data\":\"1c18297b7e65\",\"visdat\":{\"1c18297b7e65\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"],\"1c1815747091\":[\"function (y) \",\"x\"]},\"highlight\":{\"on\":\"plotly_click\",\"persistent\":false,\"dynamic\":false,\"selectize\":false,\"opacityDim\":0.20000000000000001,\"selected\":{\"opacity\":1},\"debounce\":0},\"shinyEvents\":[\"plotly_hover\",\"plotly_click\",\"plotly_selected\",\"plotly_relayout\",\"plotly_brushed\",\"plotly_brushing\",\"plotly_clickannotation\",\"plotly_doubleclick\",\"plotly_deselect\",\"plotly_afterplot\",\"plotly_sunburstclick\"],\"base_url\":\"https://plot.ly\"},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n\nStandard error of the coefficient. Note the log scale — this is not a gentle increase.\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA huge standard error produces a wide confidence interval and a p-value nowhere\nnear significance. Read casually, that says \"not enough evidence\" — the exact\nopposite of what is happening, which is that the predictor is so strongly\nassociated with the outcome that the model cannot express it.\n\n## Where the warning first appears\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nfirst_warned <- res[res$warned, ][1, ]\nknitr::kable(\n  first_warned[, c(\"beta_true\", \"estimate\", \"se\", \"overlap\")],\n  digits = 2,\n  col.names = c(\"true coefficient\", \"estimate\", \"std. error\", \"class overlap\"),\n  caption = \"The first simulated fit at which R emitted a warning.\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\nTable: The first simulated fit at which R emitted a warning.\n\n|   | true coefficient| estimate| std. error| class overlap|\n|:--|----------------:|--------:|----------:|-------------:|\n|21 |             8.45|    19.14|       5.64|          0.23|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## What this does not show\n\nThis is one predictor, one sample per setting, and no repetition — so the\nwiggle between adjacent points is sampling noise, not structure. The\nqualitative behaviour (divergence, exploding standard error) is a property of\nthe likelihood and does not depend on the seed; the specific value of `beta`\nwhere it starts does, because whether a given sample is separable is itself\nrandom.\n\nThe fix is not shown here. Penalised likelihood (Firth) and a weakly\ninformative prior both give finite estimates under separation, and comparing\nthem is its own post.\n\n## Reproducing this\n\nThis post's executed output is frozen and committed, so rendering the site\nreplays it rather than re-running it. To actually re-execute:\n\n```powershell\nquarto render posts/logistic-separation/index.qmd --no-freeze\n```\n\nDifferent numbers from a re-run would mean a package changed underneath the\npost — that is the trigger to pin dependencies.\n",
